Chapter: FactLogLayout
  cites Foreword chapter FactLog

 Every byte of each pattern is distinct so a transposition cannot survive:
 0x1234 read from a swapped pair is 0x3412 and not 0x1234.

 One measured limit is reported rather than asserted as a defect. The
 format stores a character as one byte holding its CCE code point, so it
 can only carry code points 0 to 255. CCE tier 1 begins above that, which
 is every accented letter in the twenty-seven EU languages. The last line
 prints what a tier 1 code point does on the way through, so the boundary
 is a number somebody can act on rather than a worry.

 We say:

Section: Writing test patterns

  poke-le : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  poke-le (buf) (off) (v) (n) = poke-le-loop buf off v 0 n

  poke-le-loop : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  poke-le-loop (buf) (off) (v) (i) (n) =
    if i >= n then 0
    else let b = bit-and (bit-shru v (i * 8)) 255
    in let p = poke-byte buf (off + i) b
    in poke-le-loop buf off v (i + 1) n

  pack-cce : Integer, Integer, Text, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  pack-cce (buf) (off) (txt) (i) (n) =
    if i >= n then 0
    else let p = poke-byte buf (off + i) (char-code-at txt i)
    in pack-cce buf off txt (i + 1) n

Section: The decoders against literal bytes

 The answers are hand-computed from the pattern and do not come from any
 Codex function: 0x1234 is 4660, 0x12345678 is 305419896, and
 0x0123456789ABCDEF is 81985529216486895.

  test-u16 : Text =
    let buf = alloc-bytes 64
    in let a = poke-byte buf 0 52
    in let b = poke-byte buf 1 18
    in "u16=" & show (fl-u16 buf 0) & " want=4660"

  test-u32 : Text =
    let buf = alloc-bytes 64
    in let a = poke-byte buf 0 120
    in let b = poke-byte buf 1 86
    in let c = poke-byte buf 2 52
    in let d = poke-byte buf 3 18
    in "u32=" & show (fl-u32 buf 0) & " want=305419896"

  test-u64 : Text =
    let buf = alloc-bytes 64
    in let p = poke-le buf 0 81985529216486895 8
    in "u64=" & show (fl-u64 buf 0) & " want=81985529216486895"

 All eight bytes are written, including the seven zeros, and that is
 deliberate rather than tidy. `alloc-bytes` is a bare bump -- it returns
 the heap pointer and advances it, with no zero fill -- so a buffer is
 only as clean as the memory underneath it, and a test that poked one
 byte and trusted the rest to be zero read the previous statement's
 discarded text and reported a wrong number. Write every byte you intend
 to read.

  test-u64-high : Text =
    let buf = alloc-bytes 64
    in let p = poke-le buf 0 (bit-shl 128 56) 8
    in "u64-high=" & show (fl-u64 buf 0)

Section: A field reader reads the offset its constant names

 Distinct values at each documented offset, read back through the named
 accessors. This is the identity the chapter exists to hold: kind at 32,
 timestamp at 66, content length at 74.

  test-entry-fields : Text =
    let buf = alloc-bytes 512
    in let k = poke-le buf fl-off-kind 30 2
    in let t = poke-le buf fl-off-timestamp 1234567890123 8
    in let c = poke-le buf fl-off-content-len 4321 4
    in "kind=" & show (fl-entry-kind buf) & " ts=" & show (fl-entry-timestamp buf) & " clen=" & show (fl-entry-content-len buf)

  test-superblock-fields : Text =
    let buf = alloc-bytes 512
    in let h = poke-le buf fl-off-sb-log-head 777 8
    in let g = poke-le buf fl-off-sb-index-gen 42 8
    in "log-head=" & show (fl-sb-log-head buf) & " index-gen=" & show (fl-sb-index-gen buf)

 This line, and not the round trip above it, is what pins the layout.
 Measured: drifting fl-off-content-len from 74 to 75 leaves `clen=4321`
 completely unmoved, because the test writes and reads through the same
 constant and a change moves both sides together. That is the same shape
 as a renumbered direction constant in Foreword chapter Capability, and
 it is the reason the raw values are printed at all. A round trip cannot
 see a shared constant drift; only a literal can.

  test-offsets : Text =
    "off kind=" & show fl-off-kind & " ts=" & show fl-off-timestamp & " clen=" & show fl-off-content-len & " hdr=" & show fl-header-size & " sec=" & show fl-sector-size

Section: The magic, both directions

  test-magic-ok : Text =
    let buf = alloc-bytes 512
    in let p = pack-cce buf 0 fl-magic 0 8
    in "magic-ok=" & (if fl-magic-ok buf then "yes" else "no")

  test-magic-bad : Text =
    let buf = alloc-bytes 512
    in let p = pack-cce buf 0 fl-magic 0 8
    in let corrupt = poke-byte buf 3 99
    in "magic-corrupt=" & (if fl-magic-ok buf then "yes" else "no")

Section: Sector arithmetic at the boundary

 The header is 78 bytes and a sector is 512, so a content length of 434
 exactly fills one sector and 435 must need two. An off-by-one in the
 round-up shows here and nowhere else.

  test-sectors : Text =
    "sectors 0=" & show (fl-sectors-for 0) & " 434=" & show (fl-sectors-for 434) & " 435=" & show (fl-sectors-for 435) & " 1024=" & show (fl-sectors-for 1024)

Section: Text round trip across the whole byte range

 fl-text reads one byte as one CCE code point, so the identity is that a
 byte goes in and the same number comes back out. The high byte matters
 as much as the low one: a reader that sign-extended, or that stopped at
 127, would pass on "hello" and fail here.

  test-text : Text =
    let buf = alloc-bytes 512
    in let p = pack-cce buf 16 "hello" 0 5
    in "text=" & fl-text buf 16 5

  test-text-bytes : Text =
    let buf = alloc-bytes 512
    in let a = poke-byte buf 0 200
    in let b = poke-byte buf 1 1
    in let c = poke-byte buf 2 255
    in let s = fl-text buf 0 3
    in "text-cps=" & show (char-code-at s 0) & " " & show (char-code-at s 1) & " " & show (char-code-at s 2) & " len=" & show (text-length s)

Section: Body

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni test-u16
    print-line-uni test-u32
    print-line-uni test-u64
    print-line-uni test-u64-high
    print-line-uni test-entry-fields
    print-line-uni test-superblock-fields
    print-line-uni test-offsets
    print-line-uni test-magic-ok
    print-line-uni test-magic-bad
    print-line-uni test-sectors
    print-line-uni test-text
    print-line-uni test-text-bytes
  end
